// expected: undefined: fmt.Stringer
//
// A qualified type is only legal when its package is in scope: the file imports
// it, or the signature being resolved belongs to a package whose imports we are
// carrying for a monomorphization.
//
// stage4's type resolver ended with a global by-name fallback
// (Universe.LookupByName), so `fmt.Stringer` resolved in a file that never
// imported fmt - and in a variadic parameter position the element type then came
// back nil from a diverging path, which became a `void` parameter and invalid
// IR:
//
//	%t4 = getelementptr inbounds i8, ptr %t3, i32 0   ; GEP of the slice VALUE
//	call void @main.f(void %t3, ptr null)
//
// Legacy rejects the same source with `undefined: fmt` at both sites. stage4
// now rejects it too, naming the unresolved type.
//
// The fix is NOT to remove the resolver's by-name fallback: that fallback is
// load-bearing for legitimate code (with it removed, a file that DOES import
// fmt got a nil element type from a scope that did not carry the binding, the
// slice construction computed a garbage element size, and the compiler panicked
// with "alloc: implausible size"). The rule is at the DotsType sites instead -
// a variadic parameter whose element type cannot be resolved is an error, never
// a void parameter.
package main

func f(args ...fmt.Stringer) {}

func main() {
	var s fmt.Stringer = int32(1)
	f(s)
}
